### Definition and Syntax of Interface Implementation

A class adopts an interface contract using the **`implements`** keyword. The class agrees to supply full method bodies for all abstract methods declared in the interface.

```java
class ReportGenerator implements Formattable, Exportable { ... }
```

**The Strict Visibility Rule:**
* Interface methods are implicitly `public`.
* In Java, an overriding method **cannot reduce visibility**.
* Therefore, every method implementing an interface **must explicitly be declared as `public`**.
* Omitting `public` results in default package-private visibility, which triggers an immediate compilation error: *"cannot reduce the visibility of the inherited method"*.

---

### Combining extends and implements in Code

```java
interface Printable {
    void print();
}

class BaseDocument {
    private final String documentId;

    public BaseDocument(String documentId) {
        this.documentId = documentId;
    }

    public String getDocumentId() {
        return documentId;
    }
}

// Combining single class inheritance with interface implementation
class LegalContract extends BaseDocument implements Printable {
    public LegalContract(String documentId) {
        super(documentId);
    }

    // Must be declared 'public' to fulfill interface visibility
    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing Legal Contract [ID: " + getDocumentId() + "] with notarized seals");
    }
}

public class ImplementationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LegalContract contract = new LegalContract("LC-2026-99A");
        contract.print();
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
Printing Legal Contract [ID: LC-2026-99A] with notarized seals
```

---

### Syntax Order Requirement

When combining inheritance and interfaces, always write `extends` before `implements`: `class Child extends Parent implements InterfaceA, InterfaceB`.

